{"title":"Man’s best friend?","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/970701791/0/marginalrevolution~Mans-best-friend.html","date":1790618007000,"content":"<blockquote><p>When it comes to bear encounters in or near the wild, dogs are the aggressors in 54 percent of incidents, according to a recent study conducted by bear experts at Brigham Young University and other institutions, and <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jwmg.70236?campaign=wolearlyview\" target=\"_blank\">published in the Journal of Wildlife Management</a>. In approximately 36 percent of the encounters, dogs did not come to their owners’ defense. And they only successfully alerted their owners to the presence of a bear in about 9 percent of cases.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>Here is <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/27/science/dogs-chasing-bears.html\" target=\"_blank\">more from the NYT</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/09/mans-best-friend.html\">Man’s best friend?</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/970701791/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/970701791/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/29/970701791/marginalrevolution,\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/pinterest20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/24/970701791/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/x.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/19/970701791/marginalrevolution\"><img he